+# INP diagnostics
+
+Open the page with `?inp-debug` to enable local diagnostics, then click, tap, or
+use the keyboard. In DevTools, enable **Preserve log** before following links.
+Normal visits do not load the diagnostic modules. No reports are sent anywhere.
+
+The console reports the current Interaction to Next Paint (INP) candidate, its
+target and loading state, and the input delay, event processing, and presentation
+phases. The largest phase gets a suggested investigation. Where the browser
+supports Long Animation Frames, reports include the longest overlapping script's
+URL, function, character position, invoker, and forced layout time.
+These are investigation leads, not proof that a particular script caused INP.
+
+Use `inpDiagnostics.getReports()` in the console for the latest 20 candidate
+updates or `inpDiagnostics.download()` for a JSON file. Reports live only in
+memory for this document. Export before navigating away. Selectors and script
+URLs can contain page-specific information; review exports before sharing.
+
+INP is good at ≤200 ms, needs improvement at >200–500 ms, and poor above 500 ms.
+The value is provisional until the visit ends. Callbacks report changes to the
+page's INP candidate, not every interaction. Very fast interactions can have no
+timing attribution. An empty report list means no candidate has been reported;
+it does not mean INP is zero. Unsupported browsers warn explicitly. Script
+attribution may be missing even when Event Timing is supported. This measures
+the current document; interactions inside cross-origin frames are not available.
+
+The self-contained attribution build of Google's
+[web-vitals 6.2.3](https://github.com/GoogleChrome/web-vitals) is vendored in
+`scripts/vendor` with its Apache-2.0 license. No runtime CDN or build is needed.
+See Google's [INP optimization guidance](https://web.dev/articles/optimize-inp).
